Fixes #97055 (residual cache-identity gap)

AI-assisted: this PR was prepared with Codex and reviewed with Claude.

## What Problem This Solves

The core misrouting from #97055 — custom Ollama memory providers (`ollama-cpu`, `ollama-gpu`, …) being collapsed onto the default `ollama` provider — has already landed on `main` via fa5f24b021e (adapter-side provider-id resolution) and b085184d085 (gateway-side provider-id propagation). This PR has been slimmed down to the remaining gap: the Ollama memory adapter's embedding cache identity is still only `{provider, model, outputDimensionality}`. Two configurations that reuse the same provider id but point at different endpoints (different `baseUrl`, or different non-secret custom headers selecting a different tenant/backend) hash to the same memory provider key and silently reuse stale cached embeddings from the other endpoint.

## Why This Change Was Made

The lmstudio memory adapter already keys its cache by `{provider, baseUrl, model, headers: sanitizeEmbeddingCacheHeaders(...)}`; the Ollama adapter now mirrors that pattern exactly (keeping its existing `outputDimensionality` field), so endpoint identity participates in the cache key consistently across local-server adapters.

**Secret-header hardening:** because `cacheKeyData` is hashed into the memory provider key, a credential header in it would force a full reindex on every credential rotation. `sanitizeEmbeddingCacheHeaders` in `memory-host-sdk` now always drops sensitive header names (`authorization`, `proxy-authorization`, and anything containing `api-key`/`token`/`secret`) for every adapter, and `excludedHeaderNames` becomes optional. Secret headers are still sent on the wire — only the cache identity excludes them.

## User Impact

- Users running multiple Ollama endpoints under distinct provider ids (or re-pointing one id at a new `baseUrl`) get correct, endpoint-scoped embedding caches instead of silent stale reuse.
- Rotating a credential header no longer churns the memory index, for any embedding adapter.
- **Compatibility note (maintainer acceptance item):** adding `baseUrl`/`headers` to the Ollama cache identity changes the provider-key hash for existing Ollama memory indexes (including the default `ollama` provider), so they rebuild once via the normal memory index rebuild flow on upgrade. This is intentional — it is required to distinguish endpoints, and memory indexes are rebuildable.

## Evidence

### Real-behavior proof — the exact provider cache key memory-core persists

This drives the **real** `ollamaMemoryEmbeddingProviderAdapter.create()` for ONE provider id (`ollama`) against two different endpoints, then computes the exact key memory-core writes as the index provider identity: `hashText(JSON.stringify(cacheKeyData))` (`extensions/memory-core/src/memory/manager-reindex-state.ts:55`). No mocks — real adapter, real hash.

**Before — `origin/main` @ `e43bd3d57bc`** (both endpoints collide on one key → the second endpoint silently reuses the first's cached embeddings):
```
======== REAL ollama memory embedding cache identity ========
provider id (both): ollama    model (both): nomic-embed-text

endpoint A  baseUrl=http://127.0.0.1:11434
  cacheKeyData = {"provider":"ollama","model":"nomic-embed-text"}
  providerKey  = 0eeec5c660cc1b0f3d2b83e8f758d4c80fba365db6ec183b4679534368c78085

endpoint B  baseUrl=http://10.0.0.5:11434
  cacheKeyData = {"provider":"ollama","model":"nomic-embed-text"}
  providerKey  = 0eeec5c660cc1b0f3d2b83e8f758d4c80fba365db6ec183b4679534368c78085

A vs B providerKey differ:            false
```

**After — this branch @ `850ea819308`** (each endpoint gets its own key → a change reindexes; the secret `authorization` header is stripped from the identity so rotating it does not churn the index):
```
======== REAL ollama memory embedding cache identity ========
provider id (both): ollama    model (both): nomic-embed-text

endpoint A  baseUrl=http://127.0.0.1:11434
  cacheKeyData = {"provider":"ollama","baseUrl":"http://127.0.0.1:11434","model":"nomic-embed-text","headers":[["Content-Type","application/json"]]}
  providerKey  = 780c71a6cdf63a0d574b4baf77afbf734b7bf3abf3253e177a9654a0658eb7a1

endpoint B  baseUrl=http://10.0.0.5:11434
  cacheKeyData = {"provider":"ollama","baseUrl":"http://10.0.0.5:11434","model":"nomic-embed-text","headers":[["Content-Type","application/json"]]}
  providerKey  = cd5a7580e8a73582a364a1100623226284f32e4bb8a409fae29f6887ab9919cb

endpoint A + secret authorization header
  cacheKeyData = {"provider":"ollama","baseUrl":"http://127.0.0.1:11434","model":"nomic-embed-text","headers":[["Content-Type","application/json"]]}
  providerKey  = 780c71a6cdf63a0d574b4baf77afbf734b7bf3abf3253e177a9654a0658eb7a1

A vs B providerKey differ:            true
secret token absent from cache data:  true
```

The harness asserts the two endpoints' persisted keys differ and that no secret token appears in the cache identity; it passes on this branch and fails on main (`AssertionError: expected '0eeec5c6…' not to be '0eeec5c6…'`).

<details><summary>Reproduce (drop into <code>extensions/ollama/src/</code> and <code>pnpm test</code> it on either checkout)</summary>

```ts
// Real-behavior proof for issue #97055 / PR #97059 (residual cache-identity gap).
// Drives the REAL ollama memory embedding adapter `create()` for ONE provider id
// ("ollama") against two different endpoints, then computes the EXACT provider
// cache key memory-core persists — `hashText(JSON.stringify(cacheKeyData))`
// (extensions/memory-core/src/memory/manager-reindex-state.ts:55). On this branch
// the two endpoints yield different keys (endpoint change -> reindex); on main
// they collide (endpoint change -> silent stale embedding reuse). Run:
//   pnpm test extensions/ollama/src/memory-cache-identity.proof.test.ts
import fs from "node:fs/promises";
import os from "node:os";
import path from "node:path";
import type { OpenClawConfig } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk";
import { hashText } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/memory-core-host-engine-storage";
import { expect, it } from "vitest";
import { ollamaMemoryEmbeddingProviderAdapter as adapter } from "./memory-embedding-adapter.js";

async function providerKeyFor(remote: {
  baseUrl: string;
  headers?: Record<string, string>;
}): Promise<{ cacheKeyData: Record<string, unknown>; providerKey: string }> {
  const created = await adapter.create({
    config: {} as OpenClawConfig,
    provider: "ollama", // SAME provider id for both endpoints
    model: "nomic-embed-text",
    remote,
  } as Parameters<typeof adapter.create>[0]);
  const cacheKeyData = (created.runtime.cacheKeyData ?? {}) as Record<string, unknown>;
  // Exact key memory-core writes as the index provider identity.
  const providerKey = hashText(JSON.stringify(cacheKeyData));
  return { cacheKeyData, providerKey };
}

it("PROOF #97055: same provider id, different endpoints -> distinct memory cache keys", async () => {
  const endpointA = await providerKeyFor({ baseUrl: "http://127.0.0.1:11434" });
  const endpointB = await providerKeyFor({ baseUrl: "http://10.0.0.5:11434" });
  const secretHeader = await providerKeyFor({
    baseUrl: "http://127.0.0.1:11434",
    headers: { authorization: "Bearer super-secret-token" },
  });

  const transcript = [
    "======== REAL ollama memory embedding cache identity ========",
    "provider id (both): ollama    model (both): nomic-embed-text",
    "",
    `endpoint A  baseUrl=http://127.0.0.1:11434`,
    `  cacheKeyData = ${JSON.stringify(endpointA.cacheKeyData)}`,
    `  providerKey  = ${endpointA.providerKey}`,
    "",
    `endpoint B  baseUrl=http://10.0.0.5:11434`,
    `  cacheKeyData = ${JSON.stringify(endpointB.cacheKeyData)}`,
    `  providerKey  = ${endpointB.providerKey}`,
    "",
    `endpoint A + secret authorization header`,
    `  cacheKeyData = ${JSON.stringify(secretHeader.cacheKeyData)}`,
    `  providerKey  = ${secretHeader.providerKey}`,
    "",
    `A vs B providerKey differ:            ${endpointA.providerKey !== endpointB.providerKey}`,
    `secret token absent from cache data:  ${!JSON.stringify(secretHeader.cacheKeyData).includes("super-secret-token")}`,
    "=============================================================",
  ].join("\n");
  await fs.writeFile(path.join(os.tmpdir(), "proof-97055-out.txt"), transcript, "utf8");

  // Endpoint change under one provider id must change the persisted key
  // (so memory-core reindexes instead of reusing the other endpoint's cache).
  expect(endpointA.providerKey).not.toBe(endpointB.providerKey);
  // Secret headers are never baked into the cache identity.
  expect(JSON.stringify(secretHeader.cacheKeyData)).not.toContain("super-secret-token");
});
```
</details>


### Persisted-index endpoint-switch proof — real OpenClaw CLI + agent SQLite

Run on the exact PR head `850ea819308` with Node 24.14.0 on macOS 26.5.2 arm64. I used an isolated `OPENCLAW_STATE_DIR` and the real `memory index/status/search` commands, Ollama provider adapter, memory-core manager, sqlite-vec store, and persisted `openclaw-agent.sqlite`. The external embedding boundary was two independent loopback Ollama-protocol endpoints returning deterministic 4-dimensional vectors; this is deliberately **not** presented as an Ollama-daemon/model proof.

Both configurations used the same provider id and model:

```json
{
  "provider": "ollama-proof",
  "model": "nomic-embed-text",
  "baseUrl": "http://127.0.0.1:11434"
}
```

1. `memory index --force --verbose` created the initial persisted index through endpoint A:

```text
Memory Index (main)
Provider: ollama-proof (requested: ollama-proof)
Model: nomic-embed-text
[memory] sync: indexing memory files
[memory] embeddings: batch completed
Memory index updated (main).

SQLite memory_index_meta:
providerKey=2d72ee07e689c9f6927467c4f56c42a6757a8d679b0456b61ce42150b09bc61e
vectorDims=4 chunkCount=1 embeddingCacheCount=1

endpoint A: POST /api/embed model=nomic-embed-text inputCount=1
```

2. I changed **only** `models.providers.ollama-proof.baseUrl` from port `11434` to `11435`. Before rebuilding, `memory status --deep --json` detected the persisted identity change while endpoint B was healthy:

```json
{
  "dirty": true,
  "requestedProvider": "ollama-proof",
  "custom": {
    "indexIdentity": {
      "status": "mismatched",
      "reason": "index provider settings changed"
    }
  },
  "embeddingProbe": { "ok": true }
}
```

3. A normal `memory index --verbose` (no `--force`) rebuilt through endpoint B. The persisted key changed, and the next deep status was clean:

```text
[memory] sync: indexing memory files
[memory] embeddings: batch completed
Memory index updated (main).

SQLite memory_index_meta:
providerKey=da2f0682cb541afda99daeedeb9cbe75021ae8c6a892f28e16baba3e98bbba4b
vectorDims=4 chunkCount=1 embeddingCacheCount=2

status.dirty=false
status.custom.indexIdentity.status=valid
```

4. The rebuilt index remained usable. `memory search "copper lobster observatory" --json` returned `MEMORY.md` with `vectorScore: 1` and score `0.9040008999972999`.

Request logs across the whole run were `endpoint A = 1` and `endpoint B = 4` (post-switch health probe, rebuild embedding, final health probe, and search embedding). No request returned to endpoint A after the config change. This demonstrates the exact compatibility path requested by review: an existing persisted index becomes identity-mismatched when one provider id is repointed, the normal index command rebuilds it under the new provider key, and semantic search succeeds through the new endpoint.

### Notes

The provider-id **routing** fix for #97055 already landed on `main` (fa5f24b021e, b085184d085) and is covered by main's own tests; this PR is the residual **cache-identity** parity, mirroring the lmstudio adapter. `pnpm tsgo:core` and `pnpm tsgo:extensions` are clean.

**Compatibility (maintainer acceptance item):** adding `baseUrl`/`headers` to the Ollama cache identity changes the provider-key hash for existing Ollama memory indexes, so they rebuild once via the normal memory index rebuild flow on upgrade. Intentional and required to distinguish endpoints; memory indexes are rebuildable.

Supersedes #102966 (closed: re-opened cleanly on current `main`; the bot review
state there was stale and could not be refreshed). Following this PR's review
recommendation, the recall-threshold configurability was split into
#109283 — this PR now carries the partitioning contract only.

## What Problem This Solves

#103799 isolates memories **between agents**. Within one agent's store, the
memory tools are still store-wide across every context that agent serves
(projects, people, channels):

- `memory_recall` returns the top-K nearest vectors from **every** context.
- `memory_forget` (query mode) runs a scope-blind search and can auto-delete
  **another context's copy** of a duplicated fact.
- Duplicate detection is scope-blind, so the same fact cannot be stored under
  two contexts.

The only workaround is one store per context, which defeats a shared store.

## What This Does

Adds an optional per-row **`scope`** column applied as a native LanceDB
`.where()` **prefilter** in both tools. Every scope predicate **composes with
the mandatory `agentId` fence** from #103799 into a single `.where()` clause,
built by the same `lancedb-schema.ts` helpers (`quoteLanceSqlString`) — scope
partitions *within* one agent's rows and never crosses the agent boundary.

- **Opaque partition key.** Tag a memory with a `[SCOPE:<slug>]` text prefix, or
  pass `scope` to the tools. The tag is stripped before embedding/storing (store
  tool and auto-capture; capture heuristics run on the stripped text, and a
  tag-only payload is rejected). Non-slug keys (`[A-Za-z0-9_-]+`) are rejected,
  never silently stored global. Predicates are built inside the store from the
  validated slug — no raw filter expressions from callers.
- **Partition, not a label.** Scoped recall returns its scope first (scope and
  global fetched in separate ANN passes, so strong global neighbors can't crowd
  the scope out); unscoped recall, before-prompt auto-recall, and unscoped
  `ltm search` are global-only (`--scope` is opt-in). Forget is fenced on both
  the query and `memoryId` paths. Dedup prefilters by scope, so the same fact
  can coexist under different scopes.
- **Self-healing migration** adds the column to existing tables via
  `addColumns`, preserving rows and vectors. Unlike `agentId`, scope has a safe
  default (`''` = global), so no `doctor --fix` step is needed and migrated rows
  keep today's behavior.

**Behavior-preserving until you tag:** pre-existing rows stay global; installs
that never use scope tags see no change.

**Non-goal:** scope-*targeted* auto-injection. The before-prompt hook has no
active-scope signal, so it fails safe to global-only; this PR is the foundation
for that follow-up.

**Security model:** scope is a caller-supplied partition primitive, not a trust
boundary. Today every memory is already global to all callers, so scope only
*adds* isolation; binding scopes to a trusted session identity is deferred with
the same follow-up.

## User Impact

- A tagged memory is isolated in a shared store: hidden from unscoped recall,
  never auto-recalled into an unrelated session, not deletable by an unscoped
  forget.
- `scope` on `memory_recall`/`memory_forget` reads or deletes within one
  partition safely; invalid keys are rejected instead of leaking to global.
- The same fact can live under different scopes without being swallowed as a
  duplicate.
- No scope tags → no behavior change.

## Evidence

**Real-behavior proof** — real pinned `@lancedb/lancedb` driver on an on-disk
table (no mocks), against the **current post-#103799 schema** (`agentId`
present): the scope column self-heals onto a pre-existing agent-isolated table,
preserving rows/vectors and defaulting to `''`; a scoped row sharing the **same
nearest vector** as a global row is isolated by the composed
`agentId AND scope` prefilter (not by distance ranking); the scope never
crosses the agent fence; a scoped delete is fenced. Deterministic vectors, no
network; the only slug is the fictional `demo-shop_eu`.

<details>
<summary>Proof output + reproducible script (drop at repo root, <code>node _scope-proof.mjs</code>)</summary>

```text
temp store: /tmp/mldb-scope-proof-XXXXXX

PART 1 — scope column self-heal on a pre-existing (agent-isolated) table
  pre-existing columns: id, text, vector, importance, category, createdAt, agentId
  [PASS] table has agentId (current-main shape)
  [PASS] table has no 'scope' column yet
  columns after self-heal: id, text, vector, importance, category, createdAt, agentId, scope
  [PASS] 'scope' column added
  [PASS] both pre-existing rows preserved
  [PASS] row 'a' text preserved
  [PASS] row 'a' vector preserved (len + values)
  [PASS] migrated rows default to global scope ''

PART 2 — composed agent+scope .where() prefilter isolation
  scoped recall  agent AND scope='demo-shop_eu' -> ids: [c]
  [PASS] scoped recall returns ONLY the scoped row (c)
  unscoped recall agent AND scope=''/NULL  -> ids: [a,b]
  [PASS] unscoped recall returns ONLY global rows (a,b)
  [PASS] scoped row hidden from unscoped recall despite identical nearest vector
  other-agent scoped recall                -> ids: []
  [PASS] scope never crosses the agent fence (other agent sees nothing)

PART 3 — scoped delete is fenced to its agent+scope
  rows after scoped delete -> ids: [a,b]
  [PASS] scoped delete removed the scoped row (c gone)
  [PASS] scoped delete preserved global rows (a,b remain)

=== RESULT: ALL CHECKS PASSED ===
```

```js
import { mkdtemp, rm } from "node:fs/promises";
import { tmpdir } from "node:os";
import { join } from "node:path";
import * as lancedb from "@lancedb/lancedb";

const DIM = 8;
const near = Array.from({ length: DIM }, (_, i) => (i === 0 ? 1 : 0));
const far = Array.from({ length: DIM }, (_, i) => (i === DIM - 1 ? 1 : 0));
const query = near;
const AGENT = "main";

let failures = 0;
function check(label, cond) {
  console.log(`  [${cond ? "PASS" : "FAIL"}] ${label}`);
  if (!cond) failures++;
}
const ids = (rows) => rows.map((r) => r.id).sort().join(",");

const dir = await mkdtemp(join(tmpdir(), "mldb-scope-proof-"));
const TABLE = "memories";
console.log(`temp store: ${dir}\n`);

try {
  const db = await lancedb.connect(dir);

  console.log("PART 1 — scope column self-heal on a pre-existing (agent-isolated) table");
  // Table shape as created by current main (per-agent isolation, no scope yet).
  await db.createTable(TABLE, [
    { id: "a", text: "prefers dark roast coffee", vector: near, importance: 1, category: "preference", createdAt: 1, agentId: AGENT },
    { id: "b", text: "team ships on fridays", vector: far, importance: 1, category: "fact", createdAt: 2, agentId: AGENT },
  ]);
  let tbl = await db.openTable(TABLE);

  const beforeCols = (await tbl.schema()).fields.map((f) => f.name);
  console.log(`  pre-existing columns: ${beforeCols.join(", ")}`);
  check("table has agentId (current-main shape)", beforeCols.includes("agentId"));
  check("table has no 'scope' column yet", !beforeCols.includes("scope"));

  // The store's self-heal: add the scope column with a safe global default.
  if (!beforeCols.includes("scope")) {
    await tbl.addColumns([{ name: "scope", valueSql: "''" }]);
  }

  const afterCols = (await tbl.schema()).fields.map((f) => f.name);
  console.log(`  columns after self-heal: ${afterCols.join(", ")}`);
  check("'scope' column added", afterCols.includes("scope"));

  const migratedRows = await tbl.query().toArray();
  const rowA = migratedRows.find((r) => r.id === "a");
  const rowB = migratedRows.find((r) => r.id === "b");
  check("both pre-existing rows preserved", migratedRows.length === 2 && !!rowA && !!rowB);
  check("row 'a' text preserved", rowA?.text === "prefers dark roast coffee");
  check("row 'a' vector preserved (len + values)", Array.from(rowA?.vector ?? []).join(",") === near.join(","));
  check("migrated rows default to global scope ''", (rowA?.scope ?? "") === "" && (rowB?.scope ?? "") === "");

  console.log("\nPART 2 — composed agent+scope .where() prefilter isolation");
  // 'c' is scoped and shares the SAME (nearest) vector as global 'a', so only the
  // prefilter — not distance ranking — can keep it out of an unscoped recall.
  await tbl.add([
    { id: "c", text: "demo-shop_eu catalog prices are in EUR", vector: near, importance: 1, category: "fact", createdAt: 3, agentId: AGENT, scope: "demo-shop_eu" },
  ]);

  const agentPred = `agentId = '${AGENT}'`;
  const scoped = await tbl.vectorSearch(query).where(`(${agentPred}) AND (scope = 'demo-shop_eu')`).limit(5).toArray();
  console.log(`  scoped recall  agent AND scope='demo-shop_eu' -> ids: [${ids(scoped)}]`);
  check("scoped recall returns ONLY the scoped row (c)", ids(scoped) === "c");

  const globalOnly = await tbl.vectorSearch(query).where(`(${agentPred}) AND ((scope = '' OR scope IS NULL))`).limit(5).toArray();
  console.log(`  unscoped recall agent AND scope=''/NULL  -> ids: [${ids(globalOnly)}]`);
  check("unscoped recall returns ONLY global rows (a,b)", ids(globalOnly) === "a,b");
  check("scoped row hidden from unscoped recall despite identical nearest vector", !ids(globalOnly).includes("c"));

  const otherAgent = await tbl.vectorSearch(query).where(`(agentId = 'other') AND (scope = 'demo-shop_eu')`).limit(5).toArray();
  console.log(`  other-agent scoped recall                -> ids: [${ids(otherAgent)}]`);
  check("scope never crosses the agent fence (other agent sees nothing)", otherAgent.length === 0);

  console.log("\nPART 3 — scoped delete is fenced to its agent+scope");
  await tbl.delete(`(${agentPred}) AND (scope = 'demo-shop_eu')`);
  const afterDelete = await tbl.query().toArray();
  console.log(`  rows after scoped delete -> ids: [${ids(afterDelete)}]`);
  check("scoped delete removed the scoped row (c gone)", !ids(afterDelete).includes("c"));
  check("scoped delete preserved global rows (a,b remain)", ids(afterDelete) === "a,b");

  console.log(`\n=== RESULT: ${failures === 0 ? "ALL CHECKS PASSED" : failures + " CHECK(S) FAILED"} ===`);
} finally {
  await rm(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });
}
process.exit(failures === 0 ? 0 : 1);
```

</details>

**Automated checks** — all green on this head: `tsgo` prod + test lanes, the
extensions lint shard (type-aware oxlint), the type-suppression inventory (no
`any` casts), `pnpm test:extension memory-lancedb` (**159 tests**, thirteen
scope regression tests), and the docs format/map/mdx/link checks.

<details>
<summary>The thirteen regression tests</summary>

1. `[SCOPE:<slug>]` is captured verbatim (hyphens/underscores intact) and
   stripped from the embedded input and stored text.
2. Dedup is scope-aware: the same fact survives under different scopes.
3. Dedup prefilters by scope before the top-K, so a same-scope duplicate is
   detected even among many near-identical other-scope vectors.
4. Scoped recall returns the requested scope first, even behind many strong
   global matches.
5. Unscoped recall is global-only; scoped rows never surface.
6. Scoped forget deletes only within its scope.
7. Unscoped forget cannot nominate or delete a scoped row.
8. Auto-recall injects only global memories.
9. A non-slug store tag is rejected — no silent global fallback.
10. A `memoryId` forget is fenced to the target row's scope.
11. A tag-only `[SCOPE:<slug>]` store is rejected; auto-capture skips it.
12. `ltm search` is global-only by default; `--scope <slug>` is opt-in and
    validated.
13. Auto-capture eligibility is evaluated on the scope-stripped text — the tag's
    length cannot flip a message's memory-worthiness.

</details>

## What To Review

- The composed predicate in `lancedb-store.ts` (`search`, `getScopeById`) and
  the `memoryScopePredicate` helper in `lancedb-schema.ts`: agent isolation must
  never be lost when a scope filter joins it.
- The partition fences in `extensions/memory-lancedb/index.ts`: unscoped
  recall / forget / auto-recall are global-only; the `memoryId` forget checks
  the target row's scope before deleting.
- The self-heal migration in `lancedb-store.ts` (`addColumns`, guarded) and the
  scope-aware dedup in `findCleanDuplicateMemory`.
- `docs/plugins/memory-lancedb.md`: the scope contract matches the behavior.

